                "text": "**Analyzing Dot Trajectories**\n\nI'm currently focused on the trajectory of the gray dots and their relationship to the red block. Right now, I've defined the red block's location. The initial position of one of the gray dots (0,0) suggests a diagonal movement towards it, but I'm checking to make sure that's correct, given the precise location of the red block.\n\n\n**Investigating Coordinate Movements**\n\nI'm now diving deeper into the specific coordinate movements of the dots, especially around the red block's boundaries. I see patterns emerge when observing how initial coordinates translate into output positions. My immediate focus is on the trajectory of (7,1), its final coordinate (6,1) is intriguing, considering the red block's location. I'm investigating if there's a raytracing element to the movement or if there are other rules at play.\n\n\n**Uncovering Movement Logic**\n\nI'm now zeroing in on the rules governing the dots' movement. I've re-examined the coordinates, especially in relation to the red block, and the surrounding halo. It appears a diagonal path to the block's halo may be involved, as evidenced by the outputs of (0,0), (0,9), (1,4), (9,0), and (9,9), with their final positions falling on the outer perimeter. The movement to (2,1) is no longer a consideration. The halo seems to be the target.\n\n\n**Defining Halo Mapping**\n\nI've made significant progress in defining the mapping of dot coordinates to the halo surrounding the red block. I've now precisely defined the halo's coordinates and its edges. I'm observing the specific input dots in train 1 and train 2, to map the starting dots to their final positions on the halo. Specifically, (0,0) from the top-left in train 1 now consistently maps to (2,1), the halo's top-left corner. The behavior of (7,1), its direct translation to (6,1), aligns perfectly with the halo's boundary.\n\n\n**Refining Projection Logic**\n\nI'm now refining the logic based on recent observations. My focus has shifted to projecting each dot's position to the bounding box of the halo surrounding the red block. I've been studying the output of several recent input coordinates, and now I see how dots are moving directly towards the red block. Horizontally if the row is within block row range. Vertically if the column is within the block column range. If not, the movement seems simpler. (7,1) is no longer a puzzling outlier.\n\n\n",
